`,p.jsxs(l.p,{children:[p.jsx(l.strong,{children:"How can layout algorithms be more extensible and composable?"})," Layout algorithms largely exist in silos that don't work together and can't be easily modified. For instance, I can't easily compose a graph layout algorithm like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://graphviz.org/",children:"Graphviz"})," with a reactive ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"})," framework like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://react.dev/",children:"React"}),". If I don't like how my browser justifies text, I can't easily reach into the internals and tweak its parameters. I am interested in systems like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://penrose.cs.cmu.edu/",children:"Penrose"})," (",p.jsxs(l.a,{href:"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3386569.3392375",children:[p.jsx("abbr",{children:"SIGGRAPH"})," 2020"]}),") and ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://bluefishjs.org/",children:"Bluefish"})," (",p.jsxs(l.a,{href:"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3654777.3676465",children:[p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UIST"})," 2024"]}),") that provide more extensible and compositional layout primitives."]}),`
