`,p.jsxs(l.p,{children:[p.jsxs(l.strong,{children:["How can a ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"})," programming language generate reactive updates equivalent to hand-written code?"]})," The main issue addressed by newer ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"})," frameworks is the ",p.jsx("em",{children:"performance of reactivity."})," That is, when the program's state changes, how efficiently can that change propagate to the ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"}),"? React provides an execeptionally clean and expressive model for reactivity, but it comes at a cost: its ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://legacy.reactjs.org/docs/reconciliation.html",children:"reconciliation algorithm"})," can be ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://legacy.reactjs.org/docs/optimizing-performance.html",children:"tricky to optimize"}),". Later frameworks like Solid and Svelte limit expressiveness in exchange for performance, but they still use dynamic dependency analysis (i.e., ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://www.solidjs.com/tutorial/introduction_signals",children:"signals"})," or ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://svelte.dev/blog/runes",children:"runes"}),") to which components depend on which state."]}),`
`,p.jsxs(l.p,{children:["What if you built a ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"})," language specifically around ",p.jsx(l.em,{children:"static"})," dependency analysis? I think you could generate ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"}),"s that react more efficiently than any framework today. The key question is whether such a language would be expressive enough to permit a wide range of ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"}),"s."]}),`
