`]}),p.jsxs(Bv,{summary:p.jsx(p.Fragment,{children:"But why am I, a computer scientist, doing psychology research?"}),children:[p.jsxs(l.p,{children:["Disciplinary boundaries are artificial inventions that should never stop you from doing good work! But that aside, psychology as a field prefers to deal with universal psychological phenomena (i.e., aspects of cognition that ",p.jsx(l.em,{children:"everyone"})," experiences) over domain-specific psychological phenomena. The kinds of questions I'm asking only affect a small number of experienced programmers, so it's not commonly studied in psychological research. Hence, we computer scientists have to help out!"]}),p.jsxs(l.p,{children:["Additionally, I am interested in ",p.jsx(l.em,{children:"design-relevant"})," psychology—ideas that can meaningfully influence the decisions made by programmers. Psychologists are generally not thinking about the design-relevance of their research, even if they analyze programmer behavior."]})]})]}),p.jsxs("section",{children:[p.jsx(l.h4,{children:"Project ideas"}),p.jsxs(l.p,{children:["These ideas are suitable for a strong programmer with a deep interest in psychology, cognition, and human-centered design of programs. You should (a) have enough programming experience to have a good intuition for what makes programming hard, (b) have enough experience with ",p.jsx("abbr",{children:"UI"})," tools to productively implement experimental interfaces, and (c) be prepared to read a lot of psychology books and papers."]}),p.jsxs(l.ol,{children:[`
