`]}),p.jsx(l.p,{children:"I think proof-oriented programming is crucial to advancing computer science as a field. Proofs provide guarantees that techniques like unit testing and fuzzing cannot, improving the reliability of software. More broadly, proofs require us to actually understand every piece of our software — a proof does not permit hand-waving."}),p.jsxs(l.p,{children:["Proof-oriented programming is just now becoming possible. Older proof assistants like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://coq.inria.fr/",children:"Coq"})," paved the way as shown by systems like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://compcert.org/",children:"CompCert"})," (",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://xavierleroy.org/publi/compcert-CACM.pdf",children:"2008"}),"). Newer proof assistants like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://lean-lang.org/",children:"Lean"})," and ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://www.fstar-lang.org/",children:"F*"})," are providing better devtools and compilation strategies, supporting ambitious research like ",p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://project-everest.github.io/",children:"Project Everest"}),"."]}),p.jsx(l.p,{children:"However, these languages are extremely difficult to use. Implementing a large system would generally be considered the realm of open research, not mundane engineering. The most interesting work here is often done by people with PhDs in programming language theory. As with Rust, I do not believe these techniques will truly advance the field until they can be adopted en masse."}),p.jsxs(Bv,{summary:p.jsx(p.Fragment,{children:"What about Agda / Idris / Isabelle / Dafny / Liquid Haskell / etc.?"}),children:[p.jsx(l.p,{children:"I am emphasizing Lean and F* because I am most excited about the potential of these languages. I am more excited about languages:"}),p.jsxs(l.ul,{children:[`
