`,p.jsxs(l.p,{children:[p.jsx(l.strong,{children:"How can focused skill training achieve better learning outcomes than general practice?"})," Simply practicing a skill can be less effective than doing training exercises targeted at specific learning outcomes. See: ",p.jsxs("q",{children:[p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1756-8765.2009.01053.x",children:"Perceptual Learning Modules in Mathematics"})," (2010)"]}),", ",p.jsx("q",{children:p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://psycnet.apa.org/record/1988-03299-001",children:"Sexing Day Old Chicks"})})," (1987), ",p.jsx("q",{children:p.jsx(l.a,{href:"https://psycnet.apa.org/record/1993-40718-001",children:"The Role of Deliberate Practice in the Acquisition of Expert Performance"})})," (1993). I am interested in creative ways that computers can provide training environments that are not feasible with just standard physical materials (the Perceptual Learning Modules is a good example)."]}),`
